    <desc>Change the ANSI palette, cursor, and selection colors.</desc>

  </info>

  <title>Color schemes</title>

  <p><app>Terminal</app> always uses a dark window theme and a dark terminal
  background, independently of the desktop theme. The default text and
  background colors do not follow profile settings.</p>

  <section id="custom-scheme">
    <title>Customize the palette and other colors</title>

    <p>Open <guiseq><gui style="menu">Edit</gui>
    <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui></guiseq>, select a profile, and
    open the <gui style="tab">Colors</gui> tab. You can still customize
    bold text, the cursor, and the selection highlight.</p>

    <p>Under <gui>Palette</gui>, choose a preset from <gui>Built-in schemes</gui>
    or click a palette swatch to edit an individual ANSI color. Changes
    are applied automatically.</p>

    <note style="tip">
      <p>Traditionally terminal emulators offered a 16 color palette, this is
      what you can alter here. <app>Terminal</app> supports an extended set of
      256 colors, but the additional 240 colors cannot be edited here.</p>

      <p><app>Terminal</app> even offers direct access to over 16 million
      colors, this is called “true color” mode.</p>

      <p>If the changes you make to the palette do not seem to have an effect,
      presumably the contents you see consist of such extended palette colors
      or true colors, rather than the 16 base colors.</p>
    </note>

  </section>

